<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" standalone="yes"?><rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><channel><title>2. Linear regression on SPM 405 — Machine Learning</title><link>https://ndungundegwa.github.io/spm405-notes/lecture-02/</link><description>Recent content in 2. Linear regression on SPM 405 — Machine Learning</description><generator>Hugo</generator><language>en</language><atom:link href="https://ndungundegwa.github.io/spm405-notes/lecture-02/index.xml" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><item><title>Opening</title><link>https://ndungundegwa.github.io/spm405-notes/lecture-02/opening/</link><pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://ndungundegwa.github.io/spm405-notes/lecture-02/opening/</guid><description>&lt;h1 id="opening"&gt;Opening&lt;a class="anchor" href="#opening"&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h1&gt;&#10;&lt;h2 id="slide-2"&gt;Today&lt;a class="anchor" href="#slide-2"&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&#10;&lt;blockquote class='book-hint '&gt;&#10;&lt;ol&gt;&#10;&lt;li&gt;Your group problems; 2. Simple linear regression: least squares; 3. How much to trust the result: SE, \(t\), \(R^2\); 4. Multiple linear regression; the normal equations; 5. Qualitative inputs and interactions; 6. Curves that become straight lines; 7. Gradient descent&lt;/li&gt;&#10;&lt;/ol&gt;&#10;&lt;/blockquote&gt;&lt;p&gt;The plan. Items 2–6 are ISLP Sections 3.1–3.3. Item 7 is not in ISLP; it is the method used to train models that have no formula,&#10;and we meet it here on the simplest possible case.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Simple linear regression</title><link>https://ndungundegwa.github.io/spm405-notes/lecture-02/simple-linear-regression/</link><pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://ndungundegwa.github.io/spm405-notes/lecture-02/simple-linear-regression/</guid><description>&lt;h1 id="simple-linear-regression"&gt;Simple linear regression&lt;a class="anchor" href="#simple-linear-regression"&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h1&gt;&#10;&lt;h2 id="slide-6"&gt;What is linear regression?&lt;a class="anchor" href="#slide-6"&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&#10;&lt;figure class="photo"&gt;&#10; &lt;img src="https://ndungundegwa.github.io/spm405-notes/photos/temp_sensor.jpg" alt="An LM35 temperature sensor: its output voltage rises in a straight line with temperature. Calibrating it is a linear regression." width="420" loading="lazy" /&gt;&#10; &lt;figcaption&gt;An LM35 temperature sensor: its output voltage rises in a straight line with temperature. Calibrating it is a linear regression.&#10; &lt;span class="credit"&gt;Photo: Nevit Dilmen (talk), &lt;a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:LM35_temperature_sensor_semiconductor_thermometer_1480374_5_6_HDR_enhancer.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener"&gt;Wikimedia Commons&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0" target="_blank" rel="noopener"&gt;CC BY-SA 3.0&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&#10; &lt;/figcaption&gt;&#10;&lt;/figure&gt;&#10;&#10;&lt;blockquote class='book-hint '&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;We have inputs \(X\) and a numerical output \(Y\)&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;/blockquote&gt;&lt;p&gt;Linear regression is a supervised method for regression problems: the output is a number.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Multiple linear regression</title><link>https://ndungundegwa.github.io/spm405-notes/lecture-02/multiple-linear-regression/</link><pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://ndungundegwa.github.io/spm405-notes/lecture-02/multiple-linear-regression/</guid><description>&lt;h1 id="multiple-linear-regression"&gt;Multiple linear regression&lt;a class="anchor" href="#multiple-linear-regression"&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h1&gt;&#10;&lt;h2 id="slide-15"&gt;Many inputs: multiple linear regression&lt;a class="anchor" href="#slide-15"&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&#10;&lt;figure class="photo"&gt;&#10; &lt;img src="https://ndungundegwa.github.io/spm405-notes/photos/solar_cells.jpg" alt="Photovoltaic cells: the power depends on the sunlight and on the panel temperature." width="560" loading="lazy" /&gt;&#10; &lt;figcaption&gt;Photovoltaic cells: the power depends on the sunlight and on the panel temperature.&#10; &lt;span class="credit"&gt;Photo: Department of Defense. American Forces Information Service. Defense Visual Information Center. 1994, &lt;a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_close-up_of_a_photovoltaic_cell_panel_attached_to_the_roof_of_a_house_for_an_experiment_to_convert_solar_energy_into_electricity._The_experiment_is_being_conducted_by_the_Engineer_-_DPLA_-_a08ce5d14c7c1e37beced568cc6c4968.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener"&gt;Wikimedia Commons&lt;/a&gt;, Public domain&lt;/span&gt;&#10; &lt;/figcaption&gt;&#10;&lt;/figure&gt;&#10;&#10;&lt;figure class="diagram"&gt;&#10; &lt;img src="https://ndungundegwa.github.io/spm405-notes/diagrams/solar_panel_model.svg" alt="Two inputs, one output: the solar-panel model." width="760" loading="lazy" /&gt;&#10; &lt;figcaption&gt;Two inputs, one output: the solar-panel model.&lt;/figcaption&gt;&#10;&lt;/figure&gt;&#10;&lt;blockquote class='book-hint '&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;\(Y = \beta_0 + \beta_1X_1 + \beta_2X_2 + \dots + \beta_pX_p + \varepsilon\)&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Gradient descent</title><link>https://ndungundegwa.github.io/spm405-notes/lecture-02/gradient-descent/</link><pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://ndungundegwa.github.io/spm405-notes/lecture-02/gradient-descent/</guid><description>&lt;h1 id="gradient-descent"&gt;Gradient descent&lt;a class="anchor" href="#gradient-descent"&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h1&gt;&#10;&lt;h2 id="slide-24"&gt;Loss and cost: what is the difference?&lt;a class="anchor" href="#slide-24"&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;Two words that students will meet in every later lecture and in every software library, often used loosely. Fix them now.&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;blockquote class='book-hint '&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;Loss: how wrong the model is on one data point. \(\ell_i = \frac12(y_i - \hat y_i)^2\)&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;/blockquote&gt;&lt;p&gt;The loss is a number for a single reading: half its squared residual.&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;blockquote class='book-hint '&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;Cost: the average loss over all \(n\) points. One number for the whole dataset. \(J(\bb) = \frac1n\sum_i\ell_i = \frac{1}{2n}\sum_i&#10;(y_i - \hat y_i)^2\)&lt;/p&gt;</description></item></channel></rss>